      "obligation": "refuter:6:1",
      "id": "lem-chebyshev-functions-prime-power-comparison",
      "location": "Proof step 3.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The step obtains θ(y)≤Cy only for y≥y0, then applies it to every term in “∑_{k≥3} θ(x^{1/k})≤C∑_{k≥3}x^{1/k}.” For large k, x^{1/k} can lie in [2,y0), where the stated bound has not been established. A finite-range enlargement of C would repair this, but it is neither made nor cited.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "abda9f1ebf57eb6a94efbc3a8e2d3a1a96db3f463a2b3b3b7dd416678d4b1f4c"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:2",
      "id": "lem-prime-counting-chebyshev-partial-summation",
      "location": "Proof step 4.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "From N=⌊x⌋ the step correctly notes π(x)=π(N) and θ(x)=θ(N), but these facts do not permit simply replacing N by x in θ(N)/log N+∫_2^N θ(t)/(t log²t)dt: both the boundary term and integral change. For example, with N=2 and x=5/2 they change separately. The formula remains true only after showing that the added integral cancels the boundary-term change, which the step does not do.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "574504476f26aaff0350fc2777b10beecf2c68d2c9b150a1e8f22341b44c7ff0"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:3",
      "id": "thm-bertrands-postulate",
      "location": "Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The step asserts, “Exactly as in the first step of [[thm-chebyshev-theta-linear-bounds]], ... P(N)≤4^N.” That cited proof step is invalid: at N=5 and m=2, p=3 satisfies m<p≤N but does not divide binom(5,2)=10, since (N−m)!=3! contains p. Thus the cited theorem's stated eventual linear θ bounds do not license the claimed all-N primorial estimate.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "70a3f77b86634426da2dd54353325a6cc5d08e93989ef959b173b853a2f6e7b3"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:4",
      "id": "thm-bertrands-postulate",
      "location": "Proof step 1.2",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The step is written under the theorem's only hypothesis n>1, yet claims that 2n/3<p≤n implies p²>2n and hence valuation 0. At n=2 and p=2, the interval condition holds but p²=4=2n and v₂(binom(4,2))=v₂(6)=1, not 0. A restriction excluding this case is required.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "70a3f77b86634426da2dd54353325a6cc5d08e93989ef959b173b853a2f6e7b3"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:5",
      "id": "thm-chebyshev-theta-linear-bounds",
      "location": "Proof step 1.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The step says that for m=⌊N/2⌋ every prime m<p≤N divides binom(N,m), because both denominator factorials contain only integers below p. Take N=5, m=2, p=3: (N−m)!=3! contains 3, and 3∤binom(5,2)=10. Indeed ∏_{2<p≤5}p=15>10, so the asserted divisibility and product inequality used by the induction are false.",
      "severity": "fatal",
